1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,425
Welcome to Module Thirty-One.

2
00:00:02,425 --> 00:00:06,225
Community assembly is one of ecology's oldest puzzles.

3
00:00:06,225 --> 00:00:08,762
A set of species arrives at a site.

4
00:00:08,762 --> 00:00:10,162
Some are filtered out.

5
00:00:10,162 --> 00:00:13,512
Some coexist because they use different resources.

6
00:00:13,512 --> 00:00:16,487
Some interactions hold the whole community together.

7
00:00:16,487 --> 00:00:18,125
And order matters.

8
00:00:18,125 --> 00:00:20,937
The first arrivals change what can follow.

9
00:00:20,937 --> 00:00:23,312
Today you assemble your own community.

10
00:00:23,312 --> 00:00:26,487
A router that decides how hard each job is.

11
00:00:26,487 --> 00:00:32,737
Two specialist agents, one triaging new papers against your notebook, one checking your field data.

12
00:00:32,737 --> 00:00:34,437
A memory to search.

13
00:00:34,437 --> 00:00:37,425
Verifiers that try to break every claim.

14
00:00:37,425 --> 00:00:39,812
And you, approving the final step.

15
00:00:39,812 --> 00:00:43,612
All of these parts already exist, built across this course.

16
00:00:43,612 --> 00:00:45,750
Today they become one system.

17
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:48,487
The ecological rules translate well.

18
00:00:48,487 --> 00:00:50,900
First, niche differentiation.

19
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:56,275
Each agent owns its own work, and no two agents ever write the same file.

20
00:00:56,275 --> 00:01:01,725
One source in the pack puts it neatly: if a job recurs every week, give it an agent.

21
00:01:01,725 --> 00:01:04,375
If it happens once, it's a skill.

22
00:01:04,375 --> 00:01:07,462
Second, interactions must be explicit.

23
00:01:07,462 --> 00:01:12,287
A handoff between agents is a file at a known place, with a fixed shape.

24
00:01:12,287 --> 00:01:16,812
It says who sent it, who receives it, and what done looks like for the next stage.

25
00:01:16,812 --> 00:01:24,512
And every role says what to do when the payload is incomplete, because an agent that's helpful without the facts is writing fiction.

26
00:01:24,512 --> 00:01:26,212
Third, structure.

27
00:01:26,212 --> 00:01:29,437
A course in the pack has a sentence worth memorising.

28
00:01:29,437 --> 00:01:31,662
The loop lives inside a node.

29
00:01:31,662 --> 00:01:33,912
The graph lives between nodes.

30
00:01:33,912 --> 00:01:40,937
Inside each specialist, produce, check, correct, and repeat until the check passes, at most three times.

31
00:01:40,937 --> 00:01:46,525
Between specialists, split the work, merge it with plain code, and send through a gate.

32
00:01:46,525 --> 00:01:51,975
If one piece fails, send back only that piece, with the reason and a strict scope.

33
00:01:51,975 --> 00:01:57,187
Send back the whole batch, and correct work gets rewritten and the run never settles.

34
00:01:57,187 --> 00:01:59,562
Fourth, order of arrival.

35
00:01:59,562 --> 00:02:01,700
Build the checks before the workers.

36
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:07,237
Write each check so a program can run it, and feed it a known bad record to prove it can fail.

37
00:02:07,237 --> 00:02:09,462
Here's where the metaphor breaks.

38
00:02:09,462 --> 00:02:15,375
Real communities assemble themselves, and unplanned interactions can still find a balance.

39
00:02:15,375 --> 00:02:18,387
Your system only has the interactions you build.

40
00:02:18,387 --> 00:02:21,125
An untested connection doesn't settle down.

41
00:02:21,125 --> 00:02:22,812
It fails quietly.

42
00:02:22,812 --> 00:02:29,400
In the lab, your coding agent will assemble the pipeline from your earlier builds, without rewriting what already works.

43
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:36,612
Contracts first, then checks, then roles, then the pipeline, then a map of the whole system and four small state files.

44
00:02:36,612 --> 00:02:41,062
Then a dry run on five abstracts and twenty rows of real field data.

45
00:02:41,062 --> 00:02:42,762
Finally, three tests.

46
00:02:42,762 --> 00:02:45,012
Did the output appear without you watching?

47
00:02:45,012 --> 00:02:46,962
Could a stranger understand it?

48
00:02:46,962 --> 00:02:48,862
Does a second run look the same?

49
00:02:48,862 --> 00:02:55,600
Start with Part A. Write a four-line card for each of your two specialists, and one sentence that says when a run is done.

